.TH DISPLAY.D 4 "16 November 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
display.d \- the display cards X can use, one file each
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI /etc/display.d/ card
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each display card's package puts one file in
.BR /etc/display.d ,
named for the card, that tells
.BR xstart (1)
how to find the card and run its X server.
The file is the card's own data, replaced with its package; it is not
edited.
Which card is used, and in which mode, is in
.BR /etc/display ,
not here (see
.BR display (4)).
.PP
Each line is a key and its value, separated by blanks or tabs; a
.B #
starts a comment.
A key
.B xstart
does not know is reported by
.B "xstart \-n"
as ignored, and the rest of the file is used: a card file may carry a key
that only a newer base package reads.
.TP
.BI node " path"
the card's device node, opened to see whether the card is there.
.TP
.BI probe " path \fR[\fPargument ...\fR]\fP"
a command, by its full path, that says whether the card is there, for a
card whose node alone cannot tell (see
.BR PROBES ).
Its arguments are given as written.
.TP
.BI module " name"
the kernel module that drives it, when there is one.
.TP
.BI server " path"
the X server.
.TP
.BI modes " W" x "H ..."
the screen sizes the card and its server take, the default first; or
.B auto
when the server picks the mode itself.
The card's mode list is kept here and nowhere else.
.TP
.BI depths " bits ..."
the pixel depths, the default first.
.TP
.BI args " arguments"
the server's arguments after the display number:
.B %m
is the mode,
.B %w
and
.B %h
its width and height,
.B %d
the depth,
.B %n
the display number and
.B %p
the node in use (the
.B node
line's, or the one the probe names).
.TP
.BI env " name" = "value ..."
the server's environment, with the same
.BR % -names;
it reaches the server alone, never the session's programs.
.TP
.BI limits " name" = "KB ..."
the limits raised before the server starts:
.B vmem
(the address space, for a card whose memory the server maps whole) and
.B data
(the server's own memory).
.TP
.B monsw yes \fR|\fP no
whether the card shares the monitor with the chipset display, so that the
monitor switch of
.BR display (4)
applies.
When the kernel's
.B monsw
module answers, it switches the monitor itself; otherwise the tool below
does, before the server starts and back when the session ends.
.TP
.BI monswtool " path"
that tool, given the switch value and
.B rtg
or
.BR off .
.TP
.BI restore " command"
run, by its full path, after a session that did not end with the session
itself: its server ended on its own (it crashed or was killed),
.B xinit
ended it (on a signal it caught), or
.B xstart
had to end the server because
.B xinit
was killed while the server ran (xstart(1)); never while the server still
holds the display.
For a card whose server must give the keyboard back to the console itself.
.TP
.BI once " option"
the server's option to end with its first session instead of resetting
for another, given to the server of a session
.B xstart
runs (never to
.BR xdm 's
or
.BR dtlogin 's,
which keep their server for the next login);
.B none
gives no option.
Without the line: X11R6's
.B \-terminate
for a server under
.BR /opt/amix/X11R6 ,
the system's
.B \-once
for one under
.BR /usr/X ,
nothing for another.
The system's server has no
.B \-terminate
and stops at an option it does not know.
.TP
.BI console " command"
reserved for the card's console takeover; no tool reads it yet.
.TP
.BI name " text"
the card's name in words.
.SH PROBES
With
.BR "card auto" ,
.B xstart
takes the card files in name order,
.B native
last, and uses the first card that is there; a card named in
.B /etc/display
must be there too.
A card without a
.B probe
line is there when its node opens.
A card with one is there when its probe exits 0 and then the node opens;
on any other status
.B xstart
goes on to the next card (or, for a named card, says that the probe finds
none).
.PP
The probe may print lines on its standard output, in this file's form,
which replace the file's for that run:
.TP
.BI node " path"
the node to use, for a driver whose boards share one node name with
different units;
.TP
.BI modes " W" x "H ... \fR|\fP auto"
the modes this board takes;
.TP
.BI depths " bits ..."
its depths.
.PP
Other lines are reported and ignored.
A line with a bad value makes the card unusable, and
.B xstart
says so.
.PP
For a driver package that ships a probe:
.IP \(bu 3
The probe belongs to the driver's package, not to the base package; the
base package runs it.
.IP \(bu 3
It must return at once: no waiting, no retries, no output on its standard
error when the board is merely absent.
.IP \(bu 3
It must change nothing and be safe to run at any time, as any user, with
no board fitted, and inside the installer's
.BR chroot (1M):
.B "xstart \-n"
runs it there.
.IP \(bu 3
It reads its board's identity from the driver (an information node or an
.BR ioctl (2)),
never from the node's mere opening when two boards share the node name.
.IP \(bu 3
Its standard input is
.BR /dev/null .
Only its exit status and the three lines above count.
.IP \(bu 3
When boards share a node name, the server line names the node the card
uses, outright or through
.BR %p ,
so that the server opens the board the probe chose.
.PP
The base package before 1.0-35, as on the 1.1 and 1.1.1 install media,
takes the
.B probe
line for a mistake, and those media's installers stop at it: a driver
package ships the line only with base package 1.0-35 or later.
.SH CARDS
.TP
.B native
the chipset display with the system's own X11R4 server, shipped by the base
package.
The server is monochrome and picks its own mode: 1024x768 on an A2024 or a
Moniterm, 640x480 on any other monitor.
.B SCREEN_GROUP
is the Alt-function-key screen group the server takes; it is the display
number, so
.B "xstart :10"
puts X on Alt-F10, as the system's own files do.
This card is a best effort: the server and
.B xinit
start, but on an emulated machine no session was seen to reach the server;
it may wait until its screen group is shown (Alt-F10).
.PP
The other cards' files come with their drivers' packages.
The system's A2410 and DMI Resolver servers have no file: no one has been
able to try them; their arguments are in the comments of the stock
.B Xservers
file of
.BR xdm .
.SH EXAMPLE
The Z3660's file,
.BR /etc/display.d/z3660 :
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
# /etc/display.d/z3660 -- the Z3660 RTG card (display.d(4))
name      Z3660 RTG
node      /dev/zz9000
probe     /opt/amix/bin/zz9k_probe z3660
module    z3660rtg
server    /opt/amix/X11R6/bin/Xzz9000
modes     1024x768 640x480 800x600 1280x720 1280x800 1280x1024 1920x1080
depths    16
args      -mode %m -kb
limits    vmem=131072 data=65536
monsw     yes
monswtool /opt/amix/bin/zz9k_monsw
restore   /opt/amix/bin/rtg-kbdback
.ft R
.fi
.PP
The first mode, 1024x768, is the default.
The Z3660's RTG and an MNT ZZ9000 share the node name
.BR /dev/zz9000 :
the Z3660's is always unit 0, and a ZZ9000 beside it is unit 1,
.BR /dev/zz9001 .
The probe tells the boards apart by the board identity the driver gives,
so this file is used only for a Z3660.
A ZZ9000 has two files:
.B zz9000
.RB ( "probe /opt/amix/bin/zz9k_probe zz9000 0" ),
for a ZZ9000 alone at unit 0, and
.B zz9001
.RB ( "probe /opt/amix/bin/zz9k_probe zz9000 1" ,
node
.BR /dev/zz9001 ),
for a ZZ9000 beside a Z3660, whose server line names its node with
.B "\-dev /dev/zz9001"
(the X11R6.3 package from 6.3-10 on).
For a Zorro III card the probe prints a mode list with 1920x1080.
The files name their nodes outright, without
.BR %p ,
so that they work with a base package that does not know it; later files
may use it.
A VA2000's node opens only when the board is there, so its file needs no
probe.
The server finds its fonts and its colour database by itself (the X11R6.3
package from 6.3-2 on), and links its X libraries in, so the file gives no
.BR \-fp ,
no
.B \-co
and no
.BR env .
The limits cover the card's memory, which the server maps whole, and the
server's own memory.
The monitor switch's value is in
.BR /etc/display ,
not here.
.PP
The chipset's file,
.BR /etc/display.d/native :
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
node     /dev/console
server   /usr/X/bin/X
modes    auto
depths   1
env      SCREEN_GROUP=%n
monsw    no
once     -once
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR display (4),
.BR xstart (1)
.SH NOTES
A server that resets when its last client goes takes the whole set-up
again before it can stop: on a 68LC040 or 68LC060, where X runs on the
kernel's floating-point emulation, up to half a minute.
.B xinit
waits ten seconds and then kills it, and a killed server does not give the
display back to the Amiga video.
.B once
lets the server end with the session instead.
